1. checked exception - An exception that the compiler checks at compile-time.Trying to divide by zero,
null pointer access, file not found, etc.

2. unchecked exception - An exception that the compiler does NOT check at compile-time.

| Checked Exceptions       | Unchecked Exceptions             |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
| `IOException`            | `NullPointerException`           |
| `SQLException`           | `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` |
| `FileNotFoundException`  | `IllegalArgumentException`       |
| `ClassNotFoundException` | `ArithmeticException`            |

NEW → RUNNABLE → (WAITING / TIMED_WAITING / BLOCKED) → RUNNABLE → TERMINATED


Thread is created but not started yet.

Exists before calling start().

Example:

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {});
System.out.println(t.getState()); // NEW

✅ 2. RUNNABLE

Thread is ready to run OR running.

JVM scheduler decides when it gets CPU.

Includes both:

Running

Ready-to-run (waiting for CPU)

Example:

t.start(); // moves from NEW → RUNNABLE

✅ 3. BLOCKED

Thread is waiting to acquire a monitor lock.

Happens during synchronized block/method when another thread holds the lock.

Example:

synchronized(obj) {
    // thread acquired lock
}


Another thread trying the same lock → BLOCKED.

✅ 4. WAITING

Thread is waiting indefinitely for another thread to perform an action.

Happens when using:

Object.wait()

Thread.join() (without timeout)

LockSupport.park()

Example:

synchronized(obj) {
    obj.wait(); // WAITING
}


Thread leaves WAITING when:

notify()

notifyAll()

Another thread terminates (in case of join)

✅ 5. TIMED_WAITING

Thread is waiting for a specified time.

Occurs when using:

Thread.sleep(1000)

Object.wait(1000)

Thread.join(1000)

LockSupport.parkNanos()

LockSupport.parkUntil()

Example:

Thread.sleep(3000); // TIMED_WAITING

✅ 6. TERMINATED

Thread has completed execution.

Either:

finished normally, OR

terminated due to exception

Example:

t.run(); // after completion → TERMINATED



